WEBVTT

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:07.970
Imagine waking up in a beautiful Roman
city, surrounded by busy streets,

00:00:08.020 --> 00:00:12.310
crowded marketplaces, and the sounds of
everyday life.

00:00:12.708 --> 00:00:14.538
Nobody knows what is coming.

00:00:15.383 --> 00:00:21.073
Almost two thousand years ago, Pompeii was
a thriving Roman town in the shadow of

00:00:21.123 --> 00:00:22.493
Mount Vesuvius.

00:00:22.757 --> 00:00:26.767
Its people worked, traded, celebrated, and
raised their families,

00:00:26.817 --> 00:00:30.187
just as generations had done before them.

00:00:30.547 --> 00:00:34.817
But above their city stood a volcano that
was about to change everything.

00:00:37.688 --> 00:00:42.598
Pompeii stood in the fertile region of
Campania, close to the Bay of Naples.

00:00:43.060 --> 00:00:47.090
It was a prosperous town, connected to
Roman trade and culture.

00:00:47.711 --> 00:00:51.141
Its streets were filled with shops,
workshops, public baths,

00:00:51.191 --> 00:00:52.601
and elegant houses.

00:00:52.995 --> 00:00:58.025
Some residents lived in modest homes, while
wealthy families decorated their walls

00:00:58.075 --> 00:01:01.605
with colourful frescoes and elaborate
courtyards.

00:01:01.999 --> 00:01:04.909
The city was alive with commerce and
conversation.

00:01:05.607 --> 00:01:10.117
To its residents, Mount Vesuvius was part
of the familiar landscape.

00:01:10.460 --> 00:01:14.610
They had no reason to believe their world
was about to disappear.

00:01:17.073 --> 00:01:19.403
But the mountain had a violent history.

00:01:19.822 --> 00:01:24.832
The region had experienced powerful
earthquakes, including a major earthquake

00:01:24.882 --> 00:01:30.332
about A.D. sixty-two, that damaged Pompeii
and neighbouring settlements.

00:01:30.759 --> 00:01:33.309
Repairs were still under way years later.

00:01:33.784 --> 00:01:39.574
Modern volcanology tells us that Vesuvius
was a dangerous volcanic system.

00:01:39.797 --> 00:01:45.047
Yet the people of Pompeii did not have the
scientific knowledge needed to understand

00:01:45.097 --> 00:01:47.427
what was building beneath their feet.

00:01:50.119 --> 00:01:53.289
Then came the eruption of A.D. seventy-
nine.

00:01:53.687 --> 00:01:58.617
Copies of an ancient letter traditionally
place it on the twenty-fourth of August.

00:01:58.921 --> 00:02:04.371
But archaeological evidence, including a
charcoal inscription found at Pompeii in

00:02:04.421 --> 00:02:08.871
twenty eighteen, suggests it may have
happened in the autumn instead.

00:02:09.483 --> 00:02:15.473
Mount Vesuvius violently expelled ash,
gas, and fragments of volcanic rock high

00:02:15.523 --> 00:02:16.773
into the atmosphere.

00:02:17.125 --> 00:02:20.575
A towering eruption column rose above the
mountain.

00:02:20.823 --> 00:02:23.533
Pliny the Younger, who watched from across
the bay,

00:02:23.583 --> 00:02:26.993
later compared its shape to an umbrella
pine.

00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:31.770
At first, many residents may not have
understood the scale of the danger.

00:02:32.173 --> 00:02:33.763
But the sky was changing.

00:02:34.141 --> 00:02:35.951
Fine ash began to fall.

00:02:36.273 --> 00:02:40.163
The familiar mountain had become the
centre of a catastrophe.

00:02:42.815 --> 00:02:47.925
The eruption column continued to send
volcanic material into the atmosphere.

00:02:48.368 --> 00:02:52.498
Pumice stones and ash began accumulating
across Pompeii.

00:02:52.780 --> 00:02:55.410
Daylight faded behind the falling debris.

00:02:56.019 --> 00:02:58.429
People faced impossible decisions.

00:02:58.961 --> 00:03:00.371
Some tried to escape.

00:03:00.621 --> 00:03:02.231
Others sheltered indoors.

00:03:02.818 --> 00:03:07.628
As the volcanic material piled up, roofs
and buildings came under increasing

00:03:07.678 --> 00:03:08.678
pressure.

00:03:08.860 --> 00:03:10.790
The city was no longer safe.

00:03:11.144 --> 00:03:14.034
Every passing moment made escape more
difficult.

00:03:16.525 --> 00:03:19.935
The eruption continued through the day and
into the night.

00:03:20.204 --> 00:03:25.274
Falling volcanic debris turned familiar
streets into dangerous obstacles.

00:03:25.682 --> 00:03:29.152
Buildings were damaged, and some collapsed
under the weight.

00:03:29.577 --> 00:03:31.387
Families became separated.

00:03:31.698 --> 00:03:33.348
Visibility deteriorated.

00:03:34.175 --> 00:03:39.465
The people of Pompeii were facing a
disaster unlike anything they had

00:03:40.272 --> 00:03:43.102
And the most dangerous phase was still to
come.

00:03:45.844 --> 00:03:48.894
As the eruption evolved, the danger
changed.

00:03:49.232 --> 00:03:54.782
The eruption column became unstable, and
extremely hot pyroclastic currents moved

00:03:54.832 --> 00:03:55.982
down the volcano.

00:03:56.568 --> 00:03:59.538
These were not slow rivers of molten lava.

00:03:59.821 --> 00:04:04.851
They were rapidly moving mixtures of hot
gas, ash, and volcanic fragments.

00:04:05.331 --> 00:04:09.541
Studies of the deposits suggest that
Herculaneum, closer to the volcano,

00:04:09.591 --> 00:04:11.281
was struck first.

00:04:11.604 --> 00:04:14.194
Pompeii was reached the following morning.

00:04:14.638 --> 00:04:20.028
These currents could overwhelm settlements
with extraordinary force and lethal heat.

00:04:20.308 --> 00:04:24.298
For people still in their path, the
consequences were devastating.

00:04:27.483 --> 00:04:31.653
By the time the eruption subsided, Pompeii
had been transformed.

00:04:32.031 --> 00:04:37.921
Streets, houses, shops, and public spaces
lay buried beneath volcanic deposits.

00:04:38.490 --> 00:04:42.360
Many residents had died, while others had
managed to escape.

00:04:42.759 --> 00:04:47.209
The disaster also struck neighbouring
communities, including Herculaneum,

00:04:47.259 --> 00:04:49.269
Oplontis, and Stabiae.

00:04:49.596 --> 00:04:53.726
The Roman world had witnessed a
catastrophe that would be remembered for

00:04:53.776 --> 00:04:54.776
generations.

00:04:57.606 --> 00:05:02.396
After the eruption, Pompeii gradually
disappeared from everyday knowledge.

00:05:02.745 --> 00:05:06.795
Its buildings, paintings, objects, and
streets remained buried.

00:05:07.177 --> 00:05:09.427
The city was not erased from the earth.

00:05:09.619 --> 00:05:13.129
It was preserved beneath layers of
volcanic material.

00:05:13.731 --> 00:05:19.621
Centuries passed, empires changed, and the
world around the buried town moved on.

00:05:19.980 --> 00:05:24.630
Yet beneath the ground, an extraordinary
record of Roman life remained.

00:05:27.252 --> 00:05:31.762
Centuries later, excavations began to
reveal the buried settlement.

00:05:32.052 --> 00:05:37.002
Digging at Pompeii began in seventeen
forty-eight, and in the following century,

00:05:37.052 --> 00:05:43.052
archaeologists such as Giuseppe Fiorelli
introduced more systematic methods.

00:05:43.547 --> 00:05:47.137
Researchers uncovered streets, homes,
shops, paintings,

00:05:47.187 --> 00:05:49.637
inscriptions, and everyday objects.

00:05:50.005 --> 00:05:55.235
These discoveries offered an extraordinary
window into ancient Roman society.

00:05:55.845 --> 00:05:58.775
Pompeii became more than a place of
tragedy.

00:05:59.037 --> 00:06:03.367
It became one of the most important
archaeological sites in the world.

00:06:05.818 --> 00:06:11.818
Today, Pompeii allows us to study ordinary
Roman life in remarkable detail.

00:06:12.125 --> 00:06:15.235
Its streets reveal how people moved
through the city.

00:06:15.422 --> 00:06:17.752
Its buildings show how families lived.

00:06:17.918 --> 00:06:22.388
Its paintings and inscriptions preserve
fragments of personal expression.

00:06:22.935 --> 00:06:28.245
The catastrophe that destroyed Pompeii
also left behind a unique archaeological

00:06:28.295 --> 00:06:29.305
record.

00:06:29.644 --> 00:06:35.494
Every excavated room helps us understand a
world that existed almost two thousand

00:06:35.544 --> 00:06:36.594
years ago.

00:06:38.967 --> 00:06:42.737
Pompeii was not simply a city destroyed by
a volcano.

00:06:43.096 --> 00:06:46.626
It was a community of real people, with
families, ambitions,

00:06:46.676 --> 00:06:48.546
routines, and dreams.

00:06:49.080 --> 00:06:53.690
Their lives were interrupted by one of
nature's most powerful forces.

00:06:54.364 --> 00:06:57.654
Nearly two thousand years later, their
city still speaks to us,

00:06:57.704 --> 00:07:00.794
through its streets, paintings, and ruins.

00:07:01.292 --> 00:07:05.382
It reminds us that beneath every
historical event are human stories,

00:07:05.432 --> 00:07:07.422
waiting to be uncovered.

00:07:08.369 --> 00:07:09.839
This was Pompeii.

00:07:10.328 --> 00:07:12.198
And this is Buried Chronicles.
